1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,110
This is Mars Explorer, a source-grounded
research console for planning a Marswalk

2
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:08,706
in Jezero Crater, where NASA's
Perseverance rover landed.

3
00:00:08,706 --> 00:00:13,627
Every number on screen comes from a
published NASA product, and every route is

4
00:00:13,627 --> 00:00:15,078
labeled non-certifying.

5
00:00:15,878 --> 00:00:17,917
Below the hero is real ground.

6
00:00:17,917 --> 00:00:23,286
NASA HiRISE and CTX orbital images from
Mars Trek are draped over the Mars 2020

7
00:00:23,286 --> 00:00:28,450
PLACES elevation model, and the white line
is Perseverance's published path.

8
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:30,973
Earth gets its own lane.

9
00:00:30,973 --> 00:00:36,715
This live NASA EONET feed lists wildfires,
storms, and sea ice events with their

10
00:00:36,715 --> 00:00:37,289
sources.

11
00:00:37,289 --> 00:00:40,376
Earth coordinates never touch the Mars
map.

12
00:00:41,176 --> 00:00:43,245
The challenge starts with scale.

13
00:00:43,245 --> 00:00:48,161
Here are Earth and Mars at true relative
size, tilt, and spin, from the NASA

14
00:00:48,161 --> 00:00:49,519
Planetary Fact Sheet.

15
00:00:49,519 --> 00:00:54,240
Mars is about half as wide, with
thirty-eight percent of Earth's gravity.

16
00:00:55,040 --> 00:00:56,844
This is Jezero, cell by cell.

17
00:00:56,844 --> 00:01:01,572
Each of these four thousand five hundred
columns is one sample of the PLACES

18
00:01:01,572 --> 00:01:06,486
orbital elevation model, rising from the
dark crater floor to the delta and the

19
00:01:06,486 --> 00:01:06,735
rim.

20
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:09,437
The EONET page goes deeper.

21
00:01:09,437 --> 00:01:14,581
Filter live Earth events, open a record's
timeline and sources, and check

22
00:01:14,581 --> 00:01:20,218
provenance: the upstream fetch time, the
content hash, and whether the feed came

23
00:01:20,218 --> 00:01:20,993
from cache.

24
00:01:21,793 --> 00:01:23,330
Now the mission console.

25
00:01:23,330 --> 00:01:25,444
NASA Trek layers sit on the left.

26
00:01:25,444 --> 00:01:30,439
The PLACES rover track crosses Jezero, and
the sol scrubber walks Perseverance

27
00:01:30,439 --> 00:01:32,489
through its published positions.

28
00:01:33,289 --> 00:01:35,871
Load a published route, or draw your own.

29
00:01:35,871 --> 00:01:41,161
The API samples the elevation model along
it and returns a Traverse Risk Index, with

30
00:01:41,161 --> 00:01:45,066
every component and its source listed in
the evidence cockpit.

31
00:01:45,866 --> 00:01:50,572
Under the map, the elevation and slope
profile shows the measured terrain along

32
00:01:50,572 --> 00:01:51,167
the route.

33
00:01:51,167 --> 00:01:56,171
The cockpit compares the route with where
Perseverance actually drove, and names the

34
00:01:56,171 --> 00:01:57,482
closest published sol.

35
00:01:58,282 --> 00:02:03,479
Switch to 3D, and the same route is draped
on HiRISE imagery over the elevation

36
00:02:03,479 --> 00:02:03,874
model.

37
00:02:03,874 --> 00:02:08,480
Fly route follows it across the delta,
with exaggeration you can tune.

38
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:13,928
The mission assistant answers from a local
corpus of cited NASA sources.

39
00:02:13,928 --> 00:02:18,706
Cloud models are optional and off by
default, and every answer carries its

40
00:02:18,706 --> 00:02:19,352
citations.

41
00:02:20,152 --> 00:02:25,490
Traces records each assistant run,
retrieve, grade, and assemble, so anyone

42
00:02:25,490 --> 00:02:28,337
can inspect exactly what the system did.

43
00:02:29,137 --> 00:02:33,556
The briefing turns a route into a mission
note and a server-rendered PDF.

44
00:02:33,556 --> 00:02:37,975
Below, that route is raised into an
elevation curtain, colored by sampled

45
00:02:37,975 --> 00:02:38,339
slope.

46
00:02:39,139 --> 00:02:41,470
Targets shows Perseverance's climb.

47
00:02:41,470 --> 00:02:46,797
Each pillar rises to a published PLACES
elevation, about eight hundred meters of

48
00:02:46,797 --> 00:02:49,928
ascent, with NASA-verified locations in
orange.

49
00:02:50,728 --> 00:02:52,645
Hazards maps measured slope.

50
00:02:52,645 --> 00:02:57,986
Orange cells reach fifteen degrees, where
the risk index slope term saturates.

51
00:02:57,986 --> 00:03:01,273
The grid cannot see rocks, and the page
says so.

52
00:03:02,073 --> 00:03:05,427
The mission space-time cube stacks the
traverse by sol.

53
00:03:05,427 --> 00:03:09,025
Ground position is where the rover was,
and height is when.

54
00:03:09,825 --> 00:03:14,358
The timeline computes where Earth and Mars
are on any day from NASA JPL orbital

55
00:03:14,358 --> 00:03:18,777
elements, with the distance and the
one-way light delay a command must cross.

56
00:03:19,577 --> 00:03:24,130
The data page tours all nine NASA Mars
landings, from Viking in nineteen

57
00:03:24,130 --> 00:03:27,228
seventy-six to Perseverance in twenty
twenty-one.

58
00:03:28,028 --> 00:03:33,862
Science explodes the three source layers,
elevation, imagery, and rover positions,

59
00:03:33,862 --> 00:03:37,704
and its register lists every product,
date, and limit.

60
00:03:38,504 --> 00:03:40,790
Architecture shows the real system.

61
00:03:40,790 --> 00:03:46,081
NASA sources flow through NestJS modules
into the web console, with Earth data on

62
00:03:46,081 --> 00:03:47,257
its own blue lane.

63
00:03:48,057 --> 00:03:53,324
Any route can be shared as a link and a
mission card, shown here in 3D, front and

64
00:03:53,324 --> 00:03:53,649
back.

65
00:03:54,449 --> 00:03:55,477
Mars Explorer.

66
00:03:55,477 --> 00:04:01,274
Real NASA data, cited answers, and honest
limits, from a surface question to an

67
00:04:01,274 --> 00:04:02,815
accountable briefing.
